<DocsLayout
  title="TikTok, Twitch, YouTube & Kick setup"
  description="Connect the live platforms ilyStream can read from, relay to, and monitor during a stream."
  slug="platforms"
  updated="July 2026"
  readingTime="14 min"
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Each platform has two jobs: bring events into ilyStream and, when the platform
    allows it, send chat back out. Set them up one at a time, then verify each one
    in Health Center and Chat Hub before going live.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tiktok">TikTok</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Open Connect &rarr; TikTok.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Enter your TikTok username.</strong> Use the handle without <code>@</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Start waiting mode.</strong> If you are not live yet, leave the page waiting. ilyStream checks again automatically and connects when your TikTok stream starts.</li>
    <li><strong>Capture sender credentials if you relay to TikTok.</strong> Use <em>Capture from sender</em> to pull the session values from the logged-in sender window. This avoids hand-copying cookies.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the sender account appropriate.</strong> Use the account you want viewers to see when ilyStream posts mirrored messages.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    TikTok is the most sensitive to live state. If you are not actually live, the
    connector may correctly stay in <em>Waiting to go live</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="twitch">Twitch</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Open Connect &rarr; Twitch.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Sign in with Twitch.</strong> Use the channel account or a chat bot account that has permission to talk in your channel.</li>
    <li><strong>Grant chat permissions.</strong> ilyStream needs chat read access for inbound messages and chat send access for outbound relay.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify badges and emotes.</strong> Twitch mod badges, subscriber badges, and emotes should appear in Chat Hub and Audience Stats once messages arrive.</li>
    <li><strong>Reconnect after password or token changes.</strong> If Twitch revokes the token, disconnect and sign in again.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="youtube">YouTube</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube uses Google OAuth and the YouTube Data API. Automatic live chat
    discovery can burn quota quickly if the project is new or repeatedly tested,
    so ilyStream also supports manual live video input.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Enable YouTube Data API v3.</strong> In Google Cloud, enable the API on the same project that owns your OAuth client and API key.</li>
    <li><strong>Create OAuth credentials.</strong> Use a Web application client and add <code>http://127.0.0.1:8790/callback</code> as an authorized redirect URI.</li>
    <li><strong>Add yourself as a test user.</strong> If the app is in testing mode, your Google account must be on the OAuth consent screen test-user list.</li>
    <li><strong>Paste API key, client ID, and client secret.</strong> In ilyStream, open <em>Connect &rarr; YouTube</em> and save the credentials.</li>
    <li><strong>Reconnect with Google.</strong> Approve the app in the browser and return to ilyStream.</li>
    <li><strong>Use manual mode when quota is exhausted.</strong> Paste the exact live video URL into the manual field so ilyStream does not need to search for the stream.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <span class="callout__icon">!</span>
    <p><strong>Quota is project-wide.</strong> A brand-new key in the same exhausted Google Cloud project will still be exhausted. Use a fresh project or wait for quota reset if YouTube Data API quota is spent.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kick">Kick</h2>
  <p>
    Kick event delivery is webhook-based. ilyStream listens locally, and a tunnel
    such as Cloudflared gives Kick a public HTTPS URL it can call.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Create a Kick app.</strong> In the Kick developer portal, create an app for your channel.</li>
    <li><strong>Start the ilyStream receiver.</strong> On the Kick page, confirm the local endpoint is listening, usually <code>http://127.0.0.1:8792/kick/webhook</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Start Cloudflared.</strong> Run <code>cloudflared tunnel --url http://127.0.0.1:8792</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Paste the public webhook URL.</strong> Use the Cloudflared HTTPS URL plus <code>/kick/webhook</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Save client credentials.</strong> Paste the Kick client ID, client secret, broadcaster user ID, stream URL, and stream key as needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Subscribe events.</strong> Click <em>Subscribe Events</em>. New Kick events should appear in the Kick feed and Chat Hub.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Kick outbound chat requires OAuth chat-write support. If Health Center marks
    Kick relay as read-only, inbound events can still work while sending remains disabled.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify">Verify setup</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Open Health Center.</strong> Refresh after saving each platform.</li>
    <li><strong>Run Chat Test.</strong> Make sure inbound chat lands in Chat Hub.</li>
    <li><strong>Check outbound buttons.</strong> Disabled buttons usually mean the platform is read-only or lacks chat send permission.</li>
    <li><strong>Open Audience Stats.</strong> Confirm platform icons appear in TikTok, Twitch, YouTube, Kick order.</li>
    <li><strong>Send one real message.</strong> Test from an alt account when possible so you know the live path is truly working.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <p><strong>TikTok says waiting forever.</strong> Confirm the exact username, then start the TikTok stream. TikTok will not produce live chat events while you are offline.</p>
  <p><strong>Twitch reads but cannot send.</strong> Reconnect with chat send permission and make sure the sending account is allowed to talk in the channel.</p>
  <p><strong>YouTube says quota exhausted after replacing keys.</strong> The quota belongs to the Google Cloud project. Create a new project with YouTube Data API enabled, or wait for reset.</p>
  <p><strong>Kick webhooks do not arrive.</strong> Confirm Cloudflared is still running, the public URL includes <code>/kick/webhook</code>, and the Kick app subscription uses the newest tunnel URL.</p>

